course: dictionary
lesson: 5815
title: Magnetism: an invisible pull
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Magnetism is an invisible force. A force is a push or a pull, and magnetism pulls iron and steel towards a magnet. It also makes two magnets pull or push each other. «Magnetism passes through wood, paper, plastic and water, but a thick sheet of iron soaks most of it up.» So a magnet can pull through a table without touching the metal. This is a non-contact force.
explain: Every magnet has two ends, called poles: north and south. Like poles push apart, so north and north repel. Opposite poles pull together, so north and south attract. Try it with two bar magnets and you will feel it for yourself.
explain: The Earth has its own magnetism, the Earth's magnetism. It is why a compass needle swings round to point north. Animals use it too: «Migrating birds find their way using the Sun, the stars, landmarks and the Earth's magnetism.»
explain: The word has a second meaning. Someone with «personal magnetism» has a strong charm that draws other people to them. This meaning is a comparison, not a force you could measure with a magnet.
fact: Magnetism is a force, a push or a pull that acts on things.
fact: Magnetism can act without touching: a magnet on one side of a sheet of paper can pull a paperclip on the other side.
fact: Like poles repel and opposite poles attract.
fact: «The Earth's magnetism comes from deep inside, where hot liquid iron swirls in the outer core like a giant dynamo.»
fact: Magnetism is used in fridge magnets, scrap yard cranes, speakers, motors and maglev trains.
exercise: «Magnetism passes through wood, paper, plastic and water.» What does this show about magnetism? | It makes things hot | It only works on wood | It only works when you touch it | *It works without touching things
exercise: Two bar magnets are placed north pole to north pole. What happens? | They stick together for ever | *They push apart | They melt | They attract each other
exercise: What makes a compass needle point north? | Fridge magnets | *The Earth's magnetism | Wind blowing across the ground | The Sun
test: Which of these uses magnetism? | *Fridge magnets | Baking bread | Writing with a pencil | Boiling a kettle
test: In «personal magnetism», what does magnetism mean? | A kind of rain | *A strong charm that draws people in | A type of cloud | A metal that rusts
exam: Which statement is true? | Magnetism only works when two things touch | *Magnetism can pull iron and steel without touching them | Magnetism is a type of fire | Like poles pull together
